## 3. Multi-seller Checkout & Platform Fees

When a buyer checks out with items from multiple sellers:

1. **Build an Order with Purchase Units**

   * Each unit represents one seller’s share.
   * Include a `payment_instruction.platform_fees` block to collect your marketplace commission.

2. **Capture the Order**

   * Buyer approves via PayPal; you capture funds server‐side.

<details>
<summary>Python snippet: create & capture order</summary>

```python
# 3A. Create order
order_payload = {
  "intent": "CAPTURE",
  "purchase_units": [
    {
      "amount": { "currency_code": "USD", "value": "50.00" },
      "payee":  { "merchant_id": "SELLER_MERCHANT_ID_1" },
      "payment_instruction": {
        "platform_fees": [{ "amount": { "currency_code": "USD", "value": "5.00" } }]
      }
    },
    {
      "amount": { "currency_code": "USD", "value": "75.00" },
      "payee":  { "merchant_id": "SELLER_MERCHANT_ID_2" },
      "payment_instruction": {
        "platform_fees": [{ "amount": { "currency_code": "USD", "value": "7.50" } }]
      }
    }
  ],
  "application_context": {
    "return_url": "https://yourapp.com/paypal/return",
    "cancel_url": "https://yourapp.com/paypal/cancel"
  }
}
r = requests.post(f"{API_BASE}/v2/checkout/orders", headers=hdr, json=order_payload)
order_id = r.json()["id"]

# 3B. After buyer approval, capture:
cap = requests.post(f"{API_BASE}/v2/checkout/orders/{order_id}/capture", headers=hdr)
print(cap.json())
```

</details>

